  </head>
  <body>
    <SiteHeader />

    <main>
      <section
        class="page-header pillar-hero"
        style="--topic: rgba(241,94,74,0.16); --topic-fg: #d8412c;"
      >
        <div class="container">
          <div class="breadcrumbs">
            <a href="/">Home</a>
            <span>&#8250;</span>
            <a href="/seo">SEO</a>
            <span>&#8250;</span>
            <a href="/seo/backlinks">Link Building</a>
            <span>&#8250;</span>
            <span>Canada Posting Sites</span>
          </div>

          <span class="card-icon pillar-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-maple-leaf" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
          <p class="eyebrow">Link building</p>
          <h1>{title}</h1>
          <p class="pillar-intro">{intro}</p>
          <PageByline />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Canadian publishing splits into four tiers, and where you can land a byline depends on which one you aim at and whether the story is genuinely Canadian.</p>

            <p>The national papers and broadcasters do not run guest posts. The city and regional outlets do. The business and startup titles take practitioner analysis. The lifestyle and culture magazines are more open. The list of 100 is below, grouped by tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>National news &amp; broadcasters</td><td>CBC, Globe and Mail, Toronto Star, National Post, CTV News, Global News</td><td>Not guest posts. An op-ed submission or expert commentary on a live story</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>City &amp; regional outlets</td><td>BlogTO, Daily Hive, Narcity, MTL Blog, the Village Media local network, insauga</td><td>Freelance and contributor pitches; city-specific stories, local data, guides</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Business, finance &amp; startups</td><td>Financial Post, Canadian Business, MoneySense, BetaKit, Strategy, Retail Insider</td><td>Practitioner analysis, Canadian market data, sector explainers</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Lifestyle, culture &amp; policy</td><td>Chatelaine, The Walrus, The Tyee, Cottage Life, Sharp, Policy Options</td><td>Reported features and essays with a Canadian angle</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> the Canadian market is smaller and its media has consolidated hard. A placement on a city outlet or a business title read by Canadians is worth more than a link buried in a national archive, and far more than a link on a title that has since shut down.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 Canadian sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating and estimated monthly traffic. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; closed and merged titles were swapped for live equivalents. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {SITES.map((s) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{s.n}</td>
                      <td>{s.site}</td>
                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for Canadian editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the Canadian angle.</strong> Canadian data, a provincial or federal policy hook, or a city-specific story in the first two lines.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the section or the city.</strong> "For your Toronto coverage" or "for the personal-finance desk" shows you read the outlet.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring something a staff writer cannot get.</strong> Proprietary Canadian data, a survey of Canadian users, first-hand operator experience here.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the format to the tier.</strong> Op-ed desks want a tight argument. City outlets want a local story. Business titles want a structured analysis.</li>
              <li><strong>Send one clip</strong> in a similar style.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any client or brand tie</strong> up front.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would this editor run the piece if you had nothing to sell? If yes, pitch it. If it only exists to place a link, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch CBC or the Globe a "guest post." That format does not exist there.</li>
              <li>Send a US-centric piece with "Canada" swapped in.</li>
              <li>Use the same pitch for a national title and a small-city outlet.</li>
              <li>Put exact-match anchors ("best mortgage rates Canada") on links back to your product.</li>
              <li>Chase titles that have already closed. Check the site is still publishing before you pitch.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a handful of placements on active Canadian outlets your buyers actually read will move rankings and revenue more than a long list of links, some of which point at sites that no longer exist. Use this list to find your handful.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>
              {toc.map((item) => (
                <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.text}</a></li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div id="faq">
        <Faq items={faqs} />
      </div>

      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="cta-panel" style="text-align: center; max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto;">
            <h2>Want us to run the outreach?</h2>
            <p>
              We build the shortlist by tier and city, write the pitches and the pieces with a Canadian angle,
              and place them on active Canadian publications. No link farms, no dead titles, no risk.
            </p>
            <div class="hero-actions" style="justify-content: center; margin-top: 0;">
              <a href="/services" class="button">View services</a>
              <a
                href={whatsappUrl("Hi SERP Mentor, I would like help with Canadian guest posting and digital PR.")}
                class="button-secondary"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                <i class="fa-brands fa-whatsapp" aria-hidden="true"></i> WhatsApp
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <SiteFooter />
  </body>
</html>
